###Question: T – Cell Lymphomas are associated with:
###Options:
A.  a.	HTLV Type I;
B.  b.	Skin involvement;
C.  c.	Hypercalcaemia;
D.  d.	Osteolytic bone lesion;
E.  e.	Anterior mediatinal involvement.

###Answer:

outputs:
A. a. HTLV Type I;

### Rationale:
T-cell lymphomas are a diverse group of lymphoid malignancies derived from mature T-cells. Among the options provided, Human T-lymphotropic virus type I (HTLV-I) is specifically associated with the development of adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma (ATLL), a type of T-cell lymphoma. HTLV-I is a retrovirus endemic in certain geographic regions, like parts of Africa, the Caribbean, Japan, and South America, and is known to transform T-cells leading to malignancy. While other options like skin involvement, hypercalcemia, and osteolytic bone lesions can be clinical features of certain T-cell lymphomas, HTLV Type I is a specific etiological agent linked to the pathogenesis of a T-cell lymphoma subtype.

