###Question: Group O individuals:
###Options:
A.  a.	Have A and B Antigens on their red blood cells;
B.  b.	Have Anti-A and Anti-B immunoglobulins in their sera;
C.  c.	Have no antigen(s) on their red blood cells;
D.  d.	Have no naturally occurring antibodies in their sera.

###Answer:

outputs:
B. b. Have Anti-A and Anti-B immunoglobulins in their sera;

### Rationale:
Individuals with blood group O have neither A nor B antigens on the surface of their red blood cells. Instead, their serum contains anti-A and anti-B antibodies (immunoglobulins). This means they can receive only blood from other group O individuals but can donate to any other ABO blood group because their red blood cells do not have A or B antigens that might cause a reaction. Choices A and C are incorrect because group O blood lacks A and B antigens, and choice D is incorrect as group O individuals do, in fact, have naturally occurring antibodies (anti-A and anti-B) in their serum.

